should I leave my full-time job to focus  on my business this is something that  every entrepreneur has to go through at  some point you've got a full-time job  you've got a steady paycheck but you're  not loving what you're doing you have a  dream to start your own business you've  maybe done it on the side and you want  to know when do I stop going my job stop  relying on the steady paycheck to go and  focus on my business full-time it's one  of the questions I got from one of my  YouTube viewers costaud I don't have  pronounced stuff but anyway great videos  keep up the good work I need some advice  is it important to quit my full-time job  to focus on the business so here's the  thing a lot of entrepreneurs when they  get started if they quit their full-time  job they soon discover how hard it is to  grow business growing a business is hard  for those of you who are already in it  or doing this full time we're slugging  it out you know how hard it is it's not  easy to start a business customers just  don't come lining up to you and even if  you think it's hard going in it's way  harder than you thought it was gonna be  every entrepreneur would tell you that  it's brutally hard it always takes  longer than you think it's gonna take  you don't get the reaction you think  you're gonna get and it just takes so  much time to get launched you take your  most conservative estimates at the  beginning you think I'm gonna I'm gonna  plan this out this is like my best case  scenario and this is the worst case  scenario you take your worst case  scenario and it's always worse than that  that's just a reality so be prepared for  it  a lot of entrepreneurs aren't prepared  and they start their business and they  go full-time and they quit their jobs  and they run out of money they run out  of time like you can only support  yourself for so long before you run out  of cash so you got to go do something  and they end up having to close down  their business and go back and get a job  you need to let time for momentum to  build and grow because it happens to so  many people where they've started it  they've invested so much into it they've  built some momentum they go back and get  their job and then people start to write  back and say are you still providing  this product to service and they have to  say no because they've closed down their  business you need to let time for  momentum to grow  the best way to do that is to keep your  job and have your business go on the  side to start ideally you have a job  that's also teaching you how to be a  better entrepreneur I hate getting the  job I'd never recommend getting a job  just for the money you get a job to  learn you get a job to learn how  something is done and gain experience so  when you go and do your own business  you're gonna have a lot more success so  whatever job you're in right now if it's  just for the cash you leave that I'd  join something where you're gonna learn  something work with a smaller company  where you can get more hands-on learn  from their success and give you  experience contacts so when you go and  do your own thing full time you're more  prepared for it when you're doing this  business on the side build it up to the  point where you have some degree of  confidence get it to the point where  you're at the point of survival you're  at the point where you're not making  tons of money you're not you're not  making it rich but you have enough money  to survive and even though leaving your  full-time job and going for your own  business might be a big pay cut it's  something that you're gonna do because  you love doing it and you have enough  money to least keep you afloat here's a  quick example from a company that were  invested in it's a salsa dancing company  it started off with beginning like no  students and slowly built up but now  they teach five to six thousand people a  year how to dance salsa is one of the  largest schools in North America to  learn how to dance salsa and the founder  when she started it she was doing it on  the side she was a part-time salsa  instructor and her full-time job was at  the Marriott and she was getting  promotions every year year of the year  of the year for six seven years in a row  and was on the fast track to become one  of the higher-ups at Marriott and she'd  find herself spending all day at her  Mariette job and then all night dancing  salsa cuz she loved it so much and being  super tired the next day going to her  word her job at the Marriott and it came  to the point where her business was  growing and it couldn't grow huge  because she wasn't investing her full  time into it but it was growing and she  had the opportunity to leave and she  looked at what her what her you know  what her opportunities were and she  thought I could go and do this sauce  thing nobody has ever built a massively  successful salsa business it's usually  run by artists who keep it small and and  don't know how to build a great business  and only I can do that I'm gonna go do  that and I'm gonna take a big pay cut  but I have enough there to survive I'm  not gonna have to worry about not eating  next week or not paying my electricity  bill  I have enough to survive and I'm gonna  choose that life and she was able to  build that up and to build a massively  successful salsa business and get  investors and getting instructors and  get her own studio that costs six  figures every year to maintain she was  able to do that because she poured  herself into a business but if she did  it too early if she just jumped at that  at the beginning and wouldn't have  worked because she would have made  mistakes she wouldn't have allowed time  for momentum to build she would have had  to run out of cash and go back and get  her job so summarize if you're getting a  job make sure you're doing it not just  for the money but to learn something  you're getting skills that will help you  longer-term with your business and to  start your business on the side and do  something every day to build it up even  if it's small every day you're taking  some kind of action to build your  business get it to the point while you  still have your job to where it's at  survival to where it's at the point  where you could leave your job to be  able to focus on your business and still  maintain a base standard of living not  luxury but you can survive you can pay  your bills and put food on the table and  from there with you investing your full  time into your company you have an  opportunity for massive growth that's my  favorite way the best way to will build  your business you could jump in  full-time it can work but the odds are  super low compared to if you start  part-time keep your job invest every day  to build your business and eventually  build a massive success belief you guys  like this video please give a thumbs up  below I loved seen those come in and  please leave a comment let me know what  you think you like this video leave a  message below I want to hear from you  and if you have a question for your  business leave it below in the comments  - I make a video response thank you so  much I'll see you soon  you
 

